"# Appendix — Escalation Verbs

When a Motoi program encounters something it cannot handle automatically, it does one of two things: it either surfaces the problem back up to the state machine and picks a different branch, or it hands the problem off to a human. The three verbs in this appendix are how the code says which one.

## `(domain/of kind)` — classify a task

Every task or issue carries a symbol name. `(domain/of 'payment-dispute)` returns a small record with four fields — `:domain`, `:reads`, `:via`, `:reason` — that tell the caller which team owns the problem, what state it needs to read, whether the resolution is internal or external, and a short human-readable reason.

The record is a classification, not a routing. A router reads it and picks a handler; the classifier stays out of the routing decision. In audit reviews this separation is what makes the routing traceable: you can see the classifier said `:domain payment`, and then you can see the router chose the payment handler. Two decisions, two log lines.

## `(floor/problem? kind)` — is this a floor issue?

Some problems are big enough that no automation should retry them. Payment disputes, account lockouts, data loss, legal reviews — these are \"floor-level\" and the runtime treats them as bounded-retry-exhausted from the start. `(floor/problem? 'payment-dispute)` returns `#t`; `(floor/problem? 'transient-glitch)` returns `#f`. Simple predicate, small enum.

You compose it with the router: `(when (floor/problem? kind) (route/off-domain kind))`. That's the whole idiom. If the kind is a floor issue, hand it to a human; otherwise, keep it in the automated queue.

## `(route/off-domain kind)` — hand off to a human

`route/off-domain` is fire-and-forget. It returns the symbol `'routed-off-domain` immediately and does not block for the human's response. The caller's state machine moves on; the human's resolution re-enters the system through an external event (usually a webhook or a queue message), not through the return channel of `route/off-domain`.

This is the same shape as a Unix daemon calling `setsid()` and closing its terminal, or a git branch that was pushed and deleted locally. The caller relinquishes automatic control over the task; the task can only re-enter through an external event.

## Composing them

```scheme
(define (handle kind)
  (cond ((floor/problem? kind)
         (route/off-domain kind)
         'exit-to-human)
        (else
         (auto-handler kind))))
```

That is the smallest useful escalation chain. See also Book of Composition Chapter 16 (Composing Games) for the larger pattern of graduated escalation ladders (soft retry → hard retry → route-off-domain → engineer-page).

## Also in this appendix — the ambient verbs

Two more verbs commonly appear next to the escalation trio:

- `(pattern/clave)` — return a son-clave rhythm descriptor (2-3 or 3-2). Used by scheduling code that needs a canonical five-hit pattern. Returns a record with `:kind`, `:direction`, `:map`.
- `(weather/conjure state at scale colour)` — spawn a transient atmospheric effect. Returns `'ok` on success. Fire-and-forget; the effect self-terminates.

Both are wired end-to-end. Both run through `./bin/motoi run`.
"
